---
import LegalLayout from "../layouts/LegalLayout.astro";
import { LEGAL, PAGES } from "../legal";
---

<LegalLayout
  lang="en"
  page="terms"
  title="Terms of use"
  description="The rules for using Snap2Link and Snap2Link hosting."
>
  <h2>What Snap2Link is</h2>
  <p>
    Snap2Link is a free, open source app that turns a screen capture into a link. Captures go to
    your own Google Drive, or to Snap2Link hosting, a free temporary hosting service that works
    without an account. It is run by {LEGAL.publisher}, {LEGAL.based.en}. By using Snap2Link hosting, you accept these terms.
  </p>
  <p>You must be at least 16, or have the agreement of a parent or guardian.</p>

  <h2>Snap2Link hosting</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>A link stops working after 24 hours, or after 48 hours at most with a free account. Press Delete to stop it sooner.</li>
    <li>A capture is 5 MB at most.</li>
    <li>Without an account, you can send up to 5 captures a day.</li>
    <li>
      With a free account, you can send up to 20 a day, and 6 links work at once: a new capture ends
      the link of the oldest one.
    </li>
    <li>Other limits can apply to an address or to the whole service.</li>
    <li>A security check is needed before your first upload or your first request for a sign-in code.</li>
    <li>This is not a backup: keep your own copy of anything that matters.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Your account</h2>
  <p>
    An account is optional. It needs an email address that is yours, and you sign in with a code
    sent to that address or with Google. You are responsible for what is sent from your account, and you may only
    have one. You can delete it at any time from the app.
  </p>

  <h2>Links are public</h2>
  <p>
    Anyone who has a link can see the capture, and can copy it while the link works. Check what
    is on your screen before you share it. Don't send passwords, personal data of others, or
    anything confidential.
  </p>

  <h2>Your content</h2>
  <p>
    Your captures remain yours. You let us store and serve them for as long as the link works, and
    for nothing else. You are responsible for what you send, and you must have the right to share
    it.
  </p>

  <h2>What is not allowed</h2>
  <p>You must not use Snap2Link hosting to send or share:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>child sexual abuse material, or anything that sexualizes minors;</li>
    <li>intimate images of a person without their consent;</li>
    <li>content that is illegal, that incites violence or hatred, or that harasses someone;</li>
    <li>malware, phishing, scams, or content that impersonates someone;</li>
    <li>content that infringes someone else's rights;</li>
    <li>files that aren't captures, as general file storage.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    You must not get around the limits or the security check, including with several accounts,
    automate uploads in bulk, or disrupt the service.
  </p>

  <h2>Reports and moderation</h2>
  <p>
    We don't monitor captures. Anyone can report one: see
    <a href={`${PAGES.en.legal}#report`}>how to report content</a>. If a capture breaks these terms
    or the law, we can delete it, ban the guest or account that sent it, and pass information to
    the authorities when the law requires it.
  </p>
  <p>
    When the app can show it, a refusal says why. Without an account, we usually have no way to
    contact you. If you think a decision is wrong, write to
    <a href={`mailto:${LEGAL.contact}`}>{LEGAL.contact}</a> and we will look at it again.
  </p>

  <h2>Google Drive</h2>
  <p>
    If you choose Google Drive, your captures are stored in your own account and Google's terms
    apply. We host nothing, and you decide what stays there.
  </p>

  <h2>Availability</h2>
  <p>
    Snap2Link hosting is free and provided as it is. It can be unavailable, its limits can change,
    and it can stop at any time. We give no guarantee that it will suit your needs or that a
    capture will stay available.
  </p>

  <h2>Liability</h2>
  <p>
    To the extent the law allows, we are not liable for indirect damage, loss of data, or what
    other people do with a link. Nothing here limits the rights you have as a consumer, or
    liability that the law doesn't allow us to exclude.
  </p>

  <h2>The software</h2>
  <p>
    The app's source code is available under the
    <a href="https://github.com/amys94fr/Snap2Link/blob/main/LICENSE">MIT license</a>, which
    applies to the software itself.
  </p>

  <h2>Changes and applicable law</h2>
  <p>
    If these terms change, the date at the top changes too, and the new terms apply to later use.
    These terms are governed by {LEGAL.law.en}, without affecting the mandatory consumer rights of
    the country where you live.
  </p>
  <p>Our <a href={PAGES.en.privacy}>privacy policy</a> explains how data is handled.</p>
</LegalLayout>
